<!-- Description
Scenario: A junior developer has just deployed the critical Payment Service.

Status: The Pods are Running. The Ingress is active.
The Incident: Customers are reporting 502 Bad Gateway or 503 Service Unavailable errors when trying to access payment.company.com.
The Diagnosis: The Ingress Controller cannot find any healthy backend endpoints to forward traffic to.
Your Mission:

Analyze the broken resources (Deployment and Service).
Identify the Two Fatal Configuration Errors causing the black hole.
Fix the Service configuration to restore connectivity.
Requirements:

1. Analyze:

Check the payment-app Deployment labels.
Check the payment-service Selector and Ports.
Check kubectl get endpoints payment-service (It must show an IP, currently it shows <none>).
2. Fix the Service:

Update the Service's Selector to match the Pod's labels.
Update the Service's TargetPort to match the Container's listening port.
3. Verify:

Ensure kubectl get endpoints payment-service shows an IP address.
Verify curl to payment.company.com returns "Payment Processed".
Environment:

Kubernetes Interface: kubectl is configured.
Target Namespace: default.
Tasks
Task 1: Diagnosis

Run relevant kubectl commands to understand the exact issues.
Task 2: Apply the Fix

Create a file with the corrected configuration.
Apply it to overwrite the broken service.
Task 3: Verify Connectivity

Use curl with the Host header to confirm traffic is flowing. (Replace $NODE_IP with the actual NodeIP)

curl -s -H "Host: payment.company.com" http://$NODE_IP 
Outcome
Mastered Service Discovery Debugging.
Understood the relationship between Selectors, Labels, and Endpoints.
Fixed a real-world "Traffic Black Hole". -->

# Create the corrected Service manifest
cat <<EOF > fixed-service.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: payment-service
spec:
  selector:
    app: payment     # FIXED: Matches Pod Label
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 80   # FIXED: Matches Container Port
EOF

# Apply the fix
kubectl apply -f fixed-service.yaml

Step 2: Verify

# 1. Check Endpoints (Should now show an IP)
kubectl get endpoints payment-service

# 2. Verify Connectivity
NODE_IP=$(kubectl get node -o jsonpath='{.items[0].status.addresses[0].address}')
curl -s -H "Host: payment.company.com" http://$NODE_IP | grep "Payment Processed"


